#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179504 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "364d2c9a-6e9a-57f8-aa57-c10ee223061b" :knowledge-point/title "Using the Relationship Between the Vector Field of a Function and Its Contour Plot"}] :question/id #uuid "d83c960d-aeb2-5c31-b26f-f17518220ac0" :question/math-academy-id "e-9444" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044724/assets/c4d5f211c3bf3d306c6dc14205311c60eeaaf55de89f8af571ec0fa9e5add8a6.png)\n\n \n\nWhich of the above diagrams could be the gradient vector field of the function $f(x,y) = xy?$" :question/worked-solution "We start by plotting some level curves of the function:\n\n- Setting $f(x,y) = -1,$ we get $xy = -1,$ which gives the hyperbola $y=-\\dfrac 1x$\n\n- Setting $f(x,y) = 0,$ we get $xy = 0,$ that is, the lines $y=0$ and $x=0$\n\n- Setting $f(x,y) = 1,$ we get $xy =1,$ that is, the hyperbola $y=\\dfrac 1x$\n\n- Setting $f(x,y) = -2,$ we get $xy =-2,$ that is, the hyperbola $y=-\\dfrac 2x$\n\n- $\\cdots$\n\nContinuing in this way, we get the following contour plot:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044724/assets/f95c7ab30b7b02d67ceec039792e649a900dbece2896cc1e63341fcba9fc794f.png)\n\nNote that $f(x,y)$ increases as we move from left to right in the upper half-plane $y > 0,$ and increases as we move from right-to-left in the lower half-plane $y < 0.$\n\nNow, we recall the following facts:\n\n- The gradient field vectors of a function $f(x,y)$ always lie perpendicular to its level curves.\n\n- Moreover, the vectors point in the direction where $f(x,y)$ increases.\n\nWith this in mind, we can add the vector field to the contour plot, giving the following picture.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044724/assets/3d6be27e083bb2e8b508cca31671e5cb4ea4e39b366a99f1dd194a5ea8ffcd3b.png)\n\nSo, the corresponding gradient field is as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044724/assets/996b5678396b4e9a91a4c55697442895331a7a6e4f8ec3eed550ea86845e44f4.png)\n\n       \n\nTherefore, the correct answer is III."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179506 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bd5c5fd8-409c-5b92-a0f1-4466c76cdbe0" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Gradient Vector Field of a Function"}] :question/id #uuid "2eaebefb-6b18-5689-a746-0d5dc595991c" :question/math-academy-id "e-9453" :question/problem "Find the gradient vector field of the function $f(x,y) = e^{2x-3y}.$" :question/worked-solution "The gradient vector field of a function $f$ is given by $\\nabla f.$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({e}^{2x-3y}) \\\\  & =2{e}^{2x-3y} \\\\ {f}_{y}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({e}^{2x-3y}) \\\\  & =-3{e}^{2x-3y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the gradient vector field of $f(x,y)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =2{e}^{2x-3y}\\,i-3{e}^{2x-3y}\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179511 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7a43f82e-6682-56af-994a-b0bdf12b4095" :knowledge-point/title "Sketching the Gradient Vector Field of a Function"}] :question/id #uuid "d41ebc3c-f998-577e-a50b-a08faadd561f" :question/math-academy-id "e-9474" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044724/assets/f9de4a1c2dfb6b8939b2440930eefb8b7020947bd7b943021214adf4cbfce735.png)\n\n    \n\nWhich of the above diagrams represents the gradient vector field of the function $f(x,y) = 2y^2-x^2?$" :question/worked-solution "The gradient vector field of a function $f$ is given by $\\nabla f.$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2{y}^{2}-{x}^{2}) \\\\  & =-2x \\\\ {f}_{y}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2{y}^{2}-{x}^{2}) \\\\  & =4y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the gradient vector field of $f(x,y)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =-2x\\,i+4y\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we have to sketch the vector field at the points $(2,1),$ $(-2,1),$ and $(1,-1).$\n\nTo sketch the vector field at the given points, we substitute their coordinates into the expression for $\\nabla f.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(2,1) & =-2\\cdot 2\\,i+4\\cdot 1\\,j=-4\\,i+4\\,j \\\\ ∇f(-2,1) & =-2\\cdot (-2)\\,i+4\\cdot 1\\,j=4\\,i+4\\,j \\\\ ∇f(1,-1) & =-2\\cdot 1\\,i+4\\cdot (-1)\\,j=-2\\,i-4\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we draw the vectors at their corresponding points and obtain the following diagram:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044724/assets/b88c62b154cecaace206248b62f72b8aa1f71662c0427adfbbabd5c2b4738a6b.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179896 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bd5c5fd8-409c-5b92-a0f1-4466c76cdbe0" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Gradient Vector Field of a Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8bdf2453-3eb2-5091-971a-54ba1490626b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\ln (z - 2y)i + \\frac{x}{z - 2y}j + \\frac{2x}{z - 2y}k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177377} {:answer/id #uuid "9d6b264a-e2c4-5b19-8fd2-b8a71f9540ee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\ln (z - 2y)i + \\frac{x}{2y - z}j + \\frac{x}{2y - z}k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177378} {:answer/id #uuid "a4e2a659-2ae7-5c21-8389-0401730f02f8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\ln (z - 2y)i + \\frac{2x}{z - 2y}j + \\frac{2x}{z - 2y}k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177379} {:answer/id #uuid "f37c10fc-d6f5-5508-8848-a5d7ed2cc3b3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\ln (z - 2y)i + \\frac{2x}{2y - z}j + \\frac{x}{z - 2y}k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177380} {:answer/id #uuid "3fdbad20-4755-595a-b905-15f95f29fe58" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\ln (z - 2y)i + \\frac{2x}{2y - z}j + \\frac{x}{z - 2y}k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177381}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3fdbad20-4755-595a-b905-15f95f29fe58" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\ln (z - 2y)i + \\frac{2x}{2y - z}j + \\frac{x}{z - 2y}k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177381} :answer-field/id #uuid "e7593c80-6a81-56f5-a768-6ee89e694a59" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177376}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "68db203b-e688-5d3a-9ec7-9ce2f2bf5402" :question/math-academy-id "q-122404" :question/problem "Find the gradient vector field of the function $f(x,y,z)=x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(z-2y).$" :question/worked-solution "The gradient vector field of a function $f$ is given by $∇f.$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(z-2y)) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(z-2y) \\\\ {f}_{y}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(z-2y)) \\\\  & =-\\frac{2x}{z-2y} \\\\ {f}_{z}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂z}(x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(z-2y)) \\\\  & =\\frac{x}{z-2y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the gradient vector field of $f(x,y,z)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & ={f}_{x}(x,y,z)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y,z)\\,j+{f}_{z}(x,y,z)\\,k \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(z-2y)\\,i-\\frac{2x}{z-2y}\\,j+\\frac{x}{z-2y}\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179902 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "364d2c9a-6e9a-57f8-aa57-c10ee223061b" :knowledge-point/title "Using the Relationship Between the Vector Field of a Function and Its Contour Plot"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9d1095d8-293f-5aa8-8c64-7e6dd979e26c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3692/Source/Images/q-122572-a-3.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177413} {:answer/id #uuid "ded0c347-d6b1-5426-a22f-1727a7ae3a78" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3692/Source/Images/q-122572-a-1.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177414} {:answer/id #uuid "0930e2cd-ff9c-5506-b300-1ec0f467cbec" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3692/Source/Images/q-122572-a-4.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177415} {:answer/id #uuid "274c69bb-d582-5b25-ab97-68d93734d553" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3692/Source/Images/q-122572-a-5.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177416} {:answer/id #uuid "5aabf77c-a133-59b5-80d0-3e370ccce189" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3692/Source/Images/q-122572-a-2.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177417}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ded0c347-d6b1-5426-a22f-1727a7ae3a78" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3692/Source/Images/q-122572-a-1.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177414} :answer-field/id #uuid "0e159a56-b4d8-5480-b97e-f341760dbe12" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177412}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "50f7bc31-3a53-5257-80a4-a0a85f0ea7fc" :question/math-academy-id "q-122572" :question/problem "Which of the following could be the gradient vector field of the function $f(x,y)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}?$" :question/worked-solution "We start by plotting some level curves of the function:\n\n- Setting $f(x,y)=0,$ we get ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=0,$ which gives the origin $O$\n\n- Setting $f(x,y)=1,$ we get ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1,$ a circle of radius $1$ centered at $O$\n\n- Setting $f(x,y)=4,$ we get ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=4,$ a circle of radius $2$ centered at $O$\n\n- $⋯$\n\nContinuing in this way, we get the following contour plot:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044724/assets/14c453b9fcf4670a2356b730253dd7f411b989abde303f059ebb5a9d559a4eff.png)\n\nNote that $f(x,y)$ increases as we move away from the origin.\n\nNow, we recall the following facts:\n\n- The gradient field vectors of a function $f(x,y)$ always lie perpendicular to its level curves.\n\n- Moreover, the vectors point in the direction where $f(x,y)$ increases.\n\nWith this in mind, we can add the vector field to the contour plot, giving the following picture.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044724/assets/4b20823340d89e0ff4aa2fd65078d3819e8b218419a54ea632c1109e698858ab.png)\n\nSo, the corresponding gradient field is as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044724/assets/cf6f0f97f5b54b95519319242e4f3867ae2ac0edafd5e2ab6debdfba3e519401.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323747 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bd5c5fd8-409c-5b92-a0f1-4466c76cdbe0" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Gradient Vector Field of a Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "eb00b5d5-b00f-59cb-acb2-28c38512ab8f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}}\\,i+\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}}\\,j+\\frac{z}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}}\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323749} {:answer/id #uuid "54025300-03d6-58a3-b3c9-56f1cd5ca096" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "yz\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}\\,i+xz\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}\\,j+xy\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323750} {:answer/id #uuid "1379c18b-ba1c-5d46-b926-1da867f05c97" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{yz}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}}\\,i+\\frac{xz}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}}\\,j+\\frac{xy}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}}\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323751} {:answer/id #uuid "139bdfa4-1f8c-59c7-8384-b23584b0efb9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}\\,i+2y\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}\\,j+2z\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323752} {:answer/id #uuid "3dd13b1a-105a-574d-b550-d9d4dfd0bbdc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2yz}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}\\,i+\\frac{2xz}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}\\,j+\\frac{2xy}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323753}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "eb00b5d5-b00f-59cb-acb2-28c38512ab8f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}}\\,i+\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}}\\,j+\\frac{z}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}}\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323749} :answer-field/id #uuid "1c8bc395-67b6-55b7-880b-49b60e71b422" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323748}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a300dd68-88af-507e-9066-e4497e289afb" :question/math-academy-id "q-122596" :question/problem "Find the gradient vector field of the function $f(x,y,z)=\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}.$" :question/worked-solution "The gradient vector field of a function $f$ is given by $∇f.$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}} \\\\ {f}_{y}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}} \\\\ {f}_{z}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂z}(\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{z}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the gradient vector field of $f(x,y,z)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & ={f}_{x}(x,y,z)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y,z)\\,j+{f}_{z}(x,y,z)\\,k \\\\  & =\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}}\\,i+\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}}\\,j+\\frac{z}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}}\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323754 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bd5c5fd8-409c-5b92-a0f1-4466c76cdbe0" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Gradient Vector Field of a Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cce43dcb-ff6c-5dea-9b2a-827f856310a8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(z+2y)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xz+2y)\\,i+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xz+2y)\\,j+(x+2y)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xz+2y)\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323756} {:answer/id #uuid "93b30ebb-5a2c-5602-8673-de03e4241a29" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xz+2y)\\,i+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xz+2y)\\,j+x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xz+2y)\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323757} {:answer/id #uuid "8752c14d-a01d-59fe-86c0-7feda71cd455" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xz+2y)\\,i+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xz+2y)\\,j+z\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xz+2y)\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323758} {:answer/id #uuid "df6eec77-6efd-5a21-96ac-938400b79711" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xz+2y)\\,i+2y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xz+2y)\\,j+z\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xz+2y)\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323759} {:answer/id #uuid "8230c9e9-bdc5-5860-86a7-be1d7aa0dbee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xz+2y)\\,i+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xz+2y)\\,j+x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xz+2y)\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323760}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "93b30ebb-5a2c-5602-8673-de03e4241a29" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xz+2y)\\,i+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xz+2y)\\,j+x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xz+2y)\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323757} :answer-field/id #uuid "8aa79643-983b-5cc6-9b1d-13948241112b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323755}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "cde4da3d-ec55-5f32-ac16-c33bd9acfbfc" :question/math-academy-id "q-122600" :question/problem "Find the gradient vector field of the function $f(x,y,z)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xz+2y).$" :question/worked-solution "The gradient vector field of a function $f$ is given by $∇f.$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xz+2y)) \\\\  & =z\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xz+2y) \\\\ {f}_{y}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xz+2y)) \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xz+2y) \\\\ {f}_{z}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂z}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xz+2y)) \\\\  & =x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xz+2y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the gradient vector field of $f(x,y,z)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & ={f}_{x}(x,y,z)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y,z)\\,j+{f}_{z}(x,y,z)\\,k \\\\  & =z\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xz+2y)\\,i+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xz+2y)\\,j+x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xz+2y)\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323761 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bd5c5fd8-409c-5b92-a0f1-4466c76cdbe0" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Gradient Vector Field of a Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "86df8729-4b86-5d65-9616-5b55d5563623" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2x-1}}\\,i-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{4y+1}}\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323763} {:answer/id #uuid "3e07973b-7cab-5bc0-93dc-1c33a30bd745" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{4x-1}}\\,i-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{2y+1}}\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323764} {:answer/id #uuid "7c47da1e-eb32-5bb5-8699-c2f93a2cb998" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{2x-1}}\\,i+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{4y+1}}\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323765} {:answer/id #uuid "93125115-7ba2-54bf-a769-1d3723b5435c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2x-1}}\\,i+\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{4y+1}}\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323766} {:answer/id #uuid "66d65423-8e2f-5da4-81f9-ea65d314b6f5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{4x-1}}\\,i-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2y+1}}\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323767}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "86df8729-4b86-5d65-9616-5b55d5563623" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2x-1}}\\,i-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{4y+1}}\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323763} :answer-field/id #uuid "90150a42-a85f-5de2-9664-4071fac74367" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323762}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "fc89b308-add9-53d2-a586-2eb622cde5b4" :question/math-academy-id "q-122607" :question/problem "Find the gradient vector field of the function $f(x,y)=\\sqrt{2x-1}-\\sqrt{4y+1}.$" :question/worked-solution "The gradient vector field of a function $f$ is given by $∇f.$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\sqrt{2x-1}-\\sqrt{4y+1}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2x-1}} \\\\ {f}_{y}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\sqrt{2x-1}-\\sqrt{4y+1}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{4y+1}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the gradient vector field of $f(x,y)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2x-1}}\\,i-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{4y+1}}\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323768 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bd5c5fd8-409c-5b92-a0f1-4466c76cdbe0" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Gradient Vector Field of a Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "17a82dea-a4d0-5418-8d80-ddd7fc43decd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{sec}^{2}⁡(x-2y)\\,i-{sec}^{2}⁡(x-2y)\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323770} {:answer/id #uuid "65b17c73-f96d-58b3-ba74-036e204f51ca" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x{sec}^{2}⁡(x-2y)\\,i-2y{sec}^{2}⁡(x-2y)\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323771} {:answer/id #uuid "f2724265-4796-5dcc-87a9-83ced534fcf7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sec}⁡(x-2y)\\,i-2\\operatorname{sec}⁡(x-2y)\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323772} {:answer/id #uuid "441bdf44-67a6-5746-b35e-310b1a1074ff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\operatorname{sec}⁡(x-2y)\\,i+\\operatorname{sec}⁡(x-2y)\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323773} {:answer/id #uuid "f4fbd5ae-a952-50f4-a41d-a4db7564ce14" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{sec}^{2}⁡(x-2y)\\,i-2{sec}^{2}⁡(x-2y)\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323774}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f4fbd5ae-a952-50f4-a41d-a4db7564ce14" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{sec}^{2}⁡(x-2y)\\,i-2{sec}^{2}⁡(x-2y)\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323774} :answer-field/id #uuid "1fc36121-4553-5759-80a8-37f10821ef73" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323769}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b9b6323d-ff08-50ce-be1a-fa1f61c66864" :question/math-academy-id "q-122608" :question/problem "Find the gradient vector field of the function $f(x,y)=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x-2y).$" :question/worked-solution "The gradient vector field of a function $f$ is given by $∇f.$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x-2y)) \\\\  & ={sec}^{2}⁡(x-2y) \\\\ {f}_{y}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x-2y)) \\\\  & =-2{sec}^{2}⁡(x-2y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the gradient vector field of $f(x,y)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & ={sec}^{2}⁡(x-2y)\\,i-2{sec}^{2}⁡(x-2y)\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323775 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7a43f82e-6682-56af-994a-b0bdf12b4095" :knowledge-point/title "Sketching the Gradient Vector Field of a Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fc51555c-7d4f-5501-86c8-f0532f79b1bb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044724/assets/cdebaf3d8c07fe6cb26c4fb2c5b72d2bd60dc968b3345fc1c4a5737d8c6c2b53.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323777} {:answer/id #uuid "3621f069-2b56-567e-be82-fe8d3abfd4b2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044724/assets/8a35641fa57b01a95a0c1d5b3dba8a0072b291a6822ac7b2f5e0473b9037796b.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323778} {:answer/id #uuid "5bdb5fd7-31de-52cb-86df-63085ef18703" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044724/assets/acb2066428e0660e7d4e106acd5faf60600d6f93de0b6cf4c43ee2b5864e6e51.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323779} {:answer/id #uuid "d37343ed-1795-5b3e-b35a-c57b2a1a2b01" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044724/assets/0d5f1fb1b13459937f7bd3ff0ed8b6da641fcd346dc961d7ec5b98be29e45b1d.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323780} {:answer/id #uuid "bc0d1654-e23b-5e72-a437-76d5fc83daae" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044724/assets/1461b0737bf83fee8e16c1e5e102c36c2042e46935550ebc08a0d5774a561cd3.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323781}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bc0d1654-e23b-5e72-a437-76d5fc83daae" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044724/assets/1461b0737bf83fee8e16c1e5e102c36c2042e46935550ebc08a0d5774a561cd3.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323781} :answer-field/id #uuid "291055da-c7ad-530c-bc99-a830a0c768ff" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323776}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "40689dc7-e924-59ea-9bdd-9f2acdedd9ce" :question/math-academy-id "q-122619" :question/problem "Which of the following diagrams represents the gradient vector field of the function $f(x,y)=x+{y}^{2}?$" :question/worked-solution "The gradient vector field of a function $f$ is given by $∇f.$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x+{y}^{2}) \\\\  & =1 \\\\ {f}_{y}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x+{y}^{2}) \\\\  & =2y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the gradient vector field of $f(x,y)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =\\,i+2y\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we have to sketch the vector field at the points $(-4,1),$ $(1,1),$ and $(3,-1).$\n\nTo sketch the vector field at the given points, we substitute their coordinates into the expression for $∇f.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(-4,1) & =i+2\\cdot 1\\,j=i+2\\,j \\\\ ∇f(1,1) & =i+2\\cdot 1\\,j=i+2\\,j \\\\ ∇f(3,-1) & =i+2\\cdot (-1)\\,j=i-2\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we draw the vectors starting at the corresponding points and obtain the following diagram:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044724/assets/1461b0737bf83fee8e16c1e5e102c36c2042e46935550ebc08a0d5774a561cd3.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323782 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7a43f82e-6682-56af-994a-b0bdf12b4095" :knowledge-point/title "Sketching the Gradient Vector Field of a Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3648a022-3fb7-5a0b-a02e-2822033cfc59" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044724/assets/df6472974d246217341c69163c2336190980b5aacaa5b1a5c11cbb7126ddde86.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323784} {:answer/id #uuid "d24f51e8-9ff8-51ce-9a27-8dcb023c627c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044724/assets/6c76d43daa86b2c8034e3f12592ee6c18bc533034cb255934ff7eb08b2d53623.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323785} {:answer/id #uuid "6147ad45-5d20-5769-ac9e-6d01c7d98d74" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044724/assets/92f46798ebeae9017c91ddae24e0f68cef81a1045314950bc4b56af003af0b42.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323786} {:answer/id #uuid "32448c40-e06a-58e3-be5a-38374fc25c69" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044724/assets/7618ae208ba53f12fb31988a3e0d0cb40f78ddc4348c5049bb09127039c4d965.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323787} {:answer/id #uuid "87777c72-a74c-596f-b251-14e9d73f7a3f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044724/assets/0f45b194df67a060c195d4368050cdad9b8bfefab86b41d5eb63723631543d7b.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323788}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6147ad45-5d20-5769-ac9e-6d01c7d98d74" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044724/assets/92f46798ebeae9017c91ddae24e0f68cef81a1045314950bc4b56af003af0b42.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323786} :answer-field/id #uuid "cd745c4b-76db-5536-a918-e965edb45b2b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323783}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "bef9da0c-f04d-5f81-acd5-4bdc3f69bc23" :question/math-academy-id "q-122699" :question/problem "Which of the following diagrams represents the gradient vector field of the function $f(x,y)=2y-x?$" :question/worked-solution "The gradient vector field of a function $f$ is given by $∇f.$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2y-x) \\\\  & =-1 \\\\ {f}_{y}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2y-x) \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the gradient vector field of $f(x,y)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =-i+2\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we have to sketch the vector field at the points $(-2,2),$ $(2,2),$ and $(2,-2).$\n\nTo sketch the vector field at the given points, we substitute their coordinates into the expression for $∇f.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(-2,2) & =-i+2\\,j \\\\ ∇f(2,2) & =-i+2\\,j \\\\ ∇f(2,-2) & =-i+2\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we draw the vectors starting at the corresponding points and obtain the following diagram:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044724/assets/92f46798ebeae9017c91ddae24e0f68cef81a1045314950bc4b56af003af0b42.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323789 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7a43f82e-6682-56af-994a-b0bdf12b4095" :knowledge-point/title "Sketching the Gradient Vector Field of a Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c902c70b-91c1-5148-8e26-b124c1fbcc9d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044724/assets/5e19f0874cbbfcf99c45951c98b19d941c39451c33887fbdcacdb75faeffdc57.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323791} {:answer/id #uuid "2b775338-e720-5c2e-90ff-59e0453c2ed9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044724/assets/8270942dbaed9f5177e179104bb479802a39c48afa2a528b138b458c042a1556.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323792} {:answer/id #uuid "1d2f433f-6671-52e7-944a-ec2f91e6444f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044724/assets/1da33de6ab53eee30cb7dbdba61a437fd9e3209437d061d2c546386981962942.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323793} {:answer/id #uuid "cdbcc262-d67b-597e-b50e-840d9565cbe9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044724/assets/1e9b6c550e79abfd03d4ab0c0c7942b5185d24fd2e72203d1cb87c0895e3a6f4.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323794} {:answer/id #uuid "510ae003-381e-5020-ba69-af886216d554" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044724/assets/abdb12ae2b6a67283ae65485e124b2a5264ab63a401e8993829f8b56704af479.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323795}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1d2f433f-6671-52e7-944a-ec2f91e6444f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044724/assets/1da33de6ab53eee30cb7dbdba61a437fd9e3209437d061d2c546386981962942.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323793} :answer-field/id #uuid "02af614d-c2ab-5b63-b7e7-f5ec5151a789" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323790}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "91cb20d9-0768-57d2-91f5-f873cc45139d" :question/math-academy-id "q-122713" :question/problem "Which of the following diagrams represents the gradient vector field of the function $f(x,y)=2\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(xy)?$" :question/worked-solution "The gradient vector field of a function $f$ is given by $∇f.$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(xy)) \\\\  & =\\frac{2y}{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}+1} \\\\ {f}_{y}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(xy)) \\\\  & =\\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}+1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the gradient vector field of $f(x,y)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{2y}{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}+1}\\,i+\\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}+1}\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNext, we have to sketch the vector field at the points $(-1,1),$ $(0,-2),$ and $(2,0).$\n\nTo sketch the vector field at the given points, we substitute their coordinates into the expression for $∇f.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(-1,1) & =\\frac{2\\cdot 1}{(-1)^{2}(1)^{2}+1}\\,i+\\frac{2\\cdot (-1)}{(-1)^{2}(1)^{2}+1}\\,j=i-j \\\\ ∇f(0,-2) & =\\frac{2\\cdot (-2)}{(0)^{2}(-2)^{2}+1}\\,i+\\frac{2\\cdot 0}{(0)^{2}(-2)^{2}+1}\\,j=-4\\,i \\\\ ∇f(2,0) & =\\frac{2\\cdot 0}{(2)^{2}(0)^{2}+1}\\,i+\\frac{2\\cdot 2}{(2)^{2}(0)^{2}+1}\\,j=4\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we draw the vectors starting at the corresponding points and obtain the following diagram:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044724/assets/1da33de6ab53eee30cb7dbdba61a437fd9e3209437d061d2c546386981962942.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323796 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7a43f82e-6682-56af-994a-b0bdf12b4095" :knowledge-point/title "Sketching the Gradient Vector Field of a Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8cfb882f-61ae-5fcb-b39f-f56482f72c48" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044724/assets/135122e85da455699e45c1e276152b20fee0ac9d18441eb5620b1aa43654c81b.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323798} {:answer/id #uuid "ff5b8f09-fc3a-5da3-b2ac-c024251d6116" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044724/assets/3bf6311586ae40bb490414108bbf232f40e1e727da45bbdbe3123e60c17f6268.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323799} {:answer/id #uuid "17492e81-8fc6-50ef-92c8-e5c39b447cb3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044724/assets/716f99a985d8b4909e76f922fc3bf5b3350f9cb7496ae0a0a429421016a19267.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323800} {:answer/id #uuid "a91dc664-0612-5d1a-91eb-17624ed415b1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044724/assets/2595c11a1b30fee9d76028010d2c7e608250c0d48ce0191d7da2a5412af6a110.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323801} {:answer/id #uuid "eccd7b27-c4cf-5f5c-80ae-4bd6aa53c0b6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044724/assets/e4f9011d4497ac01495c1dcde81d9ba19e9b48f9d78c36ca6259d3a2465983b7.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323802}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a91dc664-0612-5d1a-91eb-17624ed415b1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044724/assets/2595c11a1b30fee9d76028010d2c7e608250c0d48ce0191d7da2a5412af6a110.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323801} :answer-field/id #uuid "f7562ddb-6882-5063-a19d-c44e6874ae0b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323797}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f24a7d35-55d7-535e-816a-4ff2815ba4bf" :question/math-academy-id "q-122738" :question/problem "Which of the following diagrams represents the gradient vector field of the function $f(x,y)=\\frac{(y-2x)^{2}}{2}?$" :question/worked-solution "The gradient vector field of a function $f$ is given by $∇f.$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{(y-2x)^{2}}{2}) \\\\  & =-2(y-2x) \\\\ {f}_{y}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\frac{(y-2x)^{2}}{2}) \\\\  & =y-2x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the gradient vector field of $f(x,y)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =-2(y-2x)\\,i+(y-2x)\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we have to sketch the vector field at the points $(1,1),$ $(1,3),$ and $(-2,-3).$\n\nTo sketch the vector field at the given points, we substitute their coordinates into the expression for $∇f.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(1,1) & =-2\\cdot (1-2\\cdot 1)\\,i+(1-2\\cdot 1)\\,j=2\\,i-j \\\\ ∇f(1,3) & =-2\\cdot (3-2\\cdot 1)\\,i+(3-2\\cdot 1)\\,j=-2\\,i+j \\\\ ∇f(-2,-3) & =-2\\cdot (-3-2\\cdot (-2))\\,i+(-3-2\\cdot (-2))\\,j=-2\\,i+j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we draw the vectors starting at the corresponding points and obtain the following diagram:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044724/assets/2595c11a1b30fee9d76028010d2c7e608250c0d48ce0191d7da2a5412af6a110.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179904 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7a43f82e-6682-56af-994a-b0bdf12b4095" :knowledge-point/title "Sketching the Gradient Vector Field of a Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9a790d3a-b94e-5d1d-9303-3b73a27e4b99" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3692/Source/Images/q-122789-a-2.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177425} {:answer/id #uuid "9c2f9dc5-cf07-5793-832d-26b705f3b425" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3692/Source/Images/q-122789-a-3.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177426} {:answer/id #uuid "c43b933e-8503-50c8-a901-4eeb81d8198c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3692/Source/Images/q-122789-a-1.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177427} {:answer/id #uuid "20d3c5d1-b49c-599f-a2fb-0250556495af" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3692/Source/Images/q-122789-a-5.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177428} {:answer/id #uuid "382897fe-d678-5c30-9da3-98b87bea52fb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3692/Source/Images/q-122789-a-4.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177429}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c43b933e-8503-50c8-a901-4eeb81d8198c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3692/Source/Images/q-122789-a-1.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177427} :answer-field/id #uuid "d9c56295-d0c1-5c9c-be7a-71eeea125cb9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177424}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "fe3ebbef-9b0f-50cb-ab18-6df5f7b1d83c" :question/math-academy-id "q-122789" :question/problem "Which of the following diagrams represents the gradient vector field of the function $f(x,y)=\\frac{{x}^{3}-2{y}^{2}}{4}?$" :question/worked-solution "The gradient vector field of a function $f$ is given by $∇f.$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{{x}^{3}-2{y}^{2}}{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}{x}^{2} \\\\ {f}_{y}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\frac{{x}^{3}-2{y}^{2}}{4}) \\\\  & =-y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the gradient vector field of $f(x,y)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}{x}^{2}\\,i-y\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we have to sketch the vector field at the points $(-2,2),$ $(2,-3),$ and $(2,1).$\n\nTo sketch the vector field at the given points, we substitute their coordinates into the expression for $∇f.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(-2,2) & =\\frac{3}{4}\\cdot (-2)^{2}\\,i-2\\,j=3\\,i-2\\,j \\\\ ∇f(2,-3) & =\\frac{3}{4}\\cdot {2}^{2}\\,i-(-3)\\,j=3\\,i+3\\,j \\\\ ∇f(2,1) & =\\frac{3}{4}\\cdot {2}^{2}\\,i-\\,j=3\\,i-j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we draw the vectors starting at the corresponding points and obtain the following diagram:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044724/assets/26a5c0123d93231a800456e9f06f6ba7450861838c87d4d1b3ae77dab4cec6cf.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323803 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "364d2c9a-6e9a-57f8-aa57-c10ee223061b" :knowledge-point/title "Using the Relationship Between the Vector Field of a Fu