0396d5492d18199ba99a12523452db0a.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323820} {:answer/id #uuid "bf4ace8e-2ef2-5b45-9b33-575e89bf9e8e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044724/assets/57f5858f1ccae96a169ee0aac3f106bd44b3de8282412389f4eb8d3ad8b66af9.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323821} {:answer/id #uuid "a95929db-422e-51fe-bf42-d73a79fb55f4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044724/assets/c5c0cc02a032916c8f9216673f23f3db81535828b14e51889942ffaf452ac3cb.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323822} {:answer/id #uuid "d3de245e-f5b6-53b1-9617-a7aff2e2299d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044724/assets/08a6451e354eaaad23ef233a8c43d16512a2ed33257cd9e9b1257ef5eaaf558b.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323823}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7a00d1e7-85b4-568b-964d-d7f749fac776" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044724/assets/346e6bb41b8dd3b353dfedbc55eb25690396d5492d18199ba99a12523452db0a.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323820} :answer-field/id #uuid "a0456780-40a1-514d-9f84-3c653e868a7d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323818}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "ec7c19a9-6da7-55d6-b075-dc647a76929b" :question/math-academy-id "q-122834" :question/problem "Which of the following could be the gradient vector field of the function $f(x,y)={x}^{2}-{y}^{2}?$" :question/worked-solution "We start by plotting some level curves of the function:\n\n- Setting $f(x,y)=-1,$ we get ${x}^{2}-{y}^{2}=-1,$ which gives the vertical hyperbola ${y}^{2}-{x}^{2}=1$\n\n- Setting $f(x,y)=0,$ we get ${x}^{2}-{y}^{2}=0,$ which gives the pair of lines $y=\\pm x$\n\n- Setting $f(x,y)=2,$ we get ${x}^{2}-{y}^{2}=2,$ which gives the horizontal hyperbola $\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}=1$\n\n- $⋯$\n\nContinuing in this way, we get the following contour plot:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044724/assets/cecc7f28c20523aac9f153022d64da4233ce995e05df3b3e2886b7fb3b20d089.png)\n\nNote that $f(x,y)$ increases as we move from left to right in the right half-plane $x>0,$ and increases as we move from right-to-left in the left half-plane $x<0.$\n\nNow, we recall the following facts:\n\n- The gradient field vectors of a function $f(x,y)$ always lie perpendicular to its level curves.\n\n- Moreover, the vectors point in the direction where $f(x,y)$ increases.\n\nWith this in mind, we can add the vector field to the contour plot, giving the following picture.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044724/assets/037f46d3fdd190749c2aefe51222475fdd337a7b3ce53e6809048e3491a31a47.png)\n\nSo, the corresponding gradient field is as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044724/assets/346e6bb41b8dd3b353dfedbc55eb25690396d5492d18199ba99a12523452db0a.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323824 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "364d2c9a-6e9a-57f8-aa57-c10ee223061b" :knowledge-point/title "Using the Relationship Between the Vector Field of a Function and Its Contour Plot"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "36b4b992-9191-531a-92d1-a8a3daea4a4c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivari